---
success: true
exit_code: 0
----- stdout -----
pace: a local-first command-line time tracker for work and daily life

Usage: pace [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]

Commands:
  begin        Begin an entry; a running entry ends at the new start [alias: b]
  hold         Note an interruption of the running entry [alias: h]
  pause        Take a planned break from the running entry [alias: p]
  resume       Go on after a hold or a pause [alias: r]
  catch        Write a distraction down without breaking off [alias: c]
  end          End the running entry [alias: e]
  now          Show what is running, held or paused [alias: n]
  adjust       Change an entry: its start, end, note or tags; `--project` moves it [alias: a]
  log          Enter an entry afterwards
  edit         Change an entry in your editor
  undo         Take back the last command of this device
  forget       Remove an entry for good
  reflect      Look back on a period: time per role and project, targets, budgets, revenue and breaks [alias: ref]
  bill         Show the bill of a client: the billable entries of a period, priced
  export       Hand the entries of a period to other tools, one row each, as JSON or CSV
  role         Keep the roles: the lasting responsibilities time goes to
  client       Keep the clients: who gets billed, in which currency, at which rates
  project      Keep the project records: client, role, billing and budget
  setup        Walk through the settings, roles, clients and this directory's project
  config       Look at pace's configuration
  doctor       Check pace's files, entries, structure and billing, and say how to fix each problem
  store        Look after how pace keeps its data
  completions  Print the completion script for your shell
  help         Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --config <FILE>  Read the global configuration from FILE [env: PACE_CONFIG]
      --home <DIR>     Keep pace's data in DIR [env: PACE_HOME]
      --project <KEY>  The project to work on, instead of discovering it [env: PACE_PROJECT]
      --plain          Plain output: no colour, no symbols
  -h, --help           Print help
  -V, --version        Print version

----- stderr -----
